{"name":"Wheat Beer","hex":"#bf923b","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wheat beer","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A top-fermented beer brewed with a large proportion of wheat relative to the amount of malted barley."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wheat_beer"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wheat beer","blocks":[{"text":"Wheat beer is a top-fermented beer which is brewed with a large proportion of wheat relative to the amount of malted barley. The two main varieties are German Weizenbier and Belgian witbier; other types include Lambic, Berliner Weisse, and Gose."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/2013_Augustiner_Weissbier_Munich_pub.jpg/330px-2013_Augustiner_Weissbier_Munich_pub.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheat_beer","wikidata":"Q131413"},{"title":"Wheat","blocks":[{"text":"Wheat is a group of wild and domesticated grasses of the genus Triticum. As cereals, they are cultivated for their grains, which are staple foods around the world. Well-known wheat species and hybrids include the most widely grown common wheat, spelt, durum, emmer, einkorn, and Khorasan or Kamut. The archaeological record suggests that wheat was first cultivated in the regions of the Fertile Crescent around 9600 BC."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Vehn%C3%A4pelto_6.jpg/330px-Vehn%C3%A4pelto_6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheat","wikidata":"Q15645384"},{"title":"Beer","blocks":[{"text":"Beer is an alcoholic beverage produced by the brewing and fermentation of starches from cereal grain—most commonly malted barley, although wheat, maize, rice, and oats are also used. The grain is mashed to convert starch in the grain to sugars, which dissolve in water to form wort. Fermentation of the wort by yeast produces ethanol and carbonation in the beer. Beer is one of the oldest and most widely consumed alcoholic drinks in the world, and one of the most popular of all drinks. Most modern beer is brewed with hops, which add bitterness and other flavours and act as a natural preservative and stabilizer. Other flavouring agents, such as gruit, herbs, or fruits, may be included or used instead of hops. In commercial brewing, natural carbonation is often replaced with forced carbonation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Jeju_beer_09_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Jeju_beer_09_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beer","wikidata":"Q44"},{"title":"Wheat (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Wheat is a color that resembles the light yellow of the wheat grain."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Wheat_grain.jpg/330px-Wheat_grain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheat_(color)","wikidata":"Q958363"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Hefeweizen_Glass.jpg/500px-Hefeweizen_Glass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Hefeweizen_Glass.jpg/1280px-Hefeweizen_Glass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Hefeweizen_Glass.jpg/500px-Hefeweizen_Glass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A German Hefeweizen glass","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHefeweizen_Glass.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/K%C3%B6hler%27s_Medizinal-Pflanzen_in_naturgetreuen_Abbildungen_mit_kurz_erl%C3%A4uterndem_Texte_%28Plate_87%29_%288231709397%29.jpg/500px-K%C3%B6hler%27s_Medizinal-Pflanzen_i